    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="section" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
      <div class="container-narrow">
        <div class="section-head section-title-margin">
          <span class="section-head-icon"><i class="fa-solid fa-circle-question"></i></span>
          <h2 id="faq-heading" style="margin-bottom: 0;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        </div>
        <p class="section-subtitle-lean" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">Quick answers to the questions we hear most.</p>
        <div class="image-band image-band-full" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
        <img src="/images/pexels-tulou.jpg" srcset="/images/opt/pexels-tulou-400w.webp 400w, /images/opt/pexels-tulou-600w.webp 600w" sizes="100vw" alt="Historic Chinese Tulou roundhouse with red lanterns under a blue sky" width="1600" height="1067" loading="lazy">
        <div class="image-band-caption">
          <span class="band-kicker">FAQ</span>
          <span class="band-title">Still curious? Quick answers below.</span>
          <span class="band-sub">The 23 questions people ask most about Hanfu.</span>
        </div>
      </div>
        <div class="faq-list">
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What is a Hanfu dress?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Hanfu is the traditional clothing of the Han Chinese people, characterized by cross-collars, wide sleeves, and flowing robes layered with sashes. It spans over 3,000 years of history and is experiencing a global revival among enthusiasts of all backgrounds.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Can foreigners wear Hanfu?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Absolutely. Hanfu is meant to be shared and appreciated. Many Hanfu communities worldwide welcome enthusiasts of all backgrounds, and cultural appreciation is encouraged when done respectfully.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Is Hanfu different from a kimono?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Yes. Hanfu predates the kimono by centuries, using cross-collar wrapping and sash-secured robes with flowing sleeves. Kimonos feature a wide obi belt, T-shaped rectangular construction, and a more rigid silhouette.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What dynasty is Hanfu from?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Hanfu originates from the Han Dynasty (206 BCE--220 CE), but the term broadly covers traditional Han Chinese clothing from the Yellow Emperor era through the Ming Dynasty (1644 CE). Each dynasty developed distinct styles.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>How much does Hanfu cost?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Entry-level Hanfu starts around $40-80 USD for simple cotton pieces. Mid-range sets range from $100-300 USD. Premium custom-made, historically accurate silk Hanfu can cost $500-2,000+ USD depending on craftsmanship.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Can Hanfu be worn daily?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Modern Hanfu (xin hanfu) is designed for everyday wear with simplified construction, comfortable breathable fabrics, and practical silhouettes. Traditional ceremonial Hanfu is better suited for festivals, weddings, and special occasions.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Where can I buy authentic Hanfu?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Reputable sellers include Hanfu Story, NewMoonDance, Nuwa Hanfu, and curated Etsy shops. Always check reviews, material composition, and size charts before purchasing. For authentic Chinese sellers, Taobao and Xianyu offer the widest selection.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What size Hanfu should I get?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Hanfu sizing follows Chinese measurements, which often run smaller than Western sizes. Always measure your bust, waist, hips, and height, then consult the seller's specific size chart. Many Hanfu pieces are adjustable thanks to sash-and-tie construction.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>How do I wash Hanfu?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Silk Hanfu should be hand-washed in cold water with mild detergent or dry cleaned. Cotton and polyester-blend Hanfu can usually be machine-washed on a gentle cycle in a mesh bag. Always air dry flat and iron on low heat.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What accessories go with Hanfu?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Traditional accessories include hairpins (zan), jade pendants, decorative fans, embroidered sachets, tassels, and waist ornaments. The choice depends on the dynasty style, occasion, and season. For formal events, hair crowns complete the look.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Is Hanfu comfortable?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Yes, Hanfu is known for its comfort. The loose, flowing cuts allow freedom of movement, and natural fabrics like cotton, linen, and silk breathe well in warm weather. Modern Hanfu often uses lightweight, wrinkle-resistant blends.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What is the difference between Hanfu and Qipao?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Hanfu is loose-fitting, layered, and has roots stretching back thousands of years. Qipao (cheongsam) is a form-fitting, 20th-century dress originating in 1920s Shanghai, influenced by Manchu qizhuang clothing and Western tailoring techniques.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Can I wear Hanfu to a wedding?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Absolutely, Hanfu is becoming increasingly popular for weddings, both in China and internationally. Red is the traditional bridal color symbolizing joy and prosperity. Many couples now opt for Tang or Ming dynasty-style wedding Hanfu.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What shoes to wear with Hanfu?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Traditional embroidered cloth shoes with flat or low heels are the standard choice. For casual modern Hanfu, simple flats or neutral-toned sneakers can work. Historical reenactors often wear specific period footwear like cloud-head shoes.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>How to choose your first Hanfu?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Start with a simple modern Hanfu (xin hanfu) in cotton or linen for everyday wear. Choose a versatile color like navy, black, or soft pastel. The Ming dynasty style is beginner-friendly because of its structured cuts and adjustable waist ties.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Is Hanfu unisex?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Historically, Hanfu had distinct men's and women's styles, but many modern designs are gender-inclusive. Men's Hanfu typically features straighter cuts and darker colors, while women's styles are more flowing with brighter colors and embroidery.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What is modern Hanfu?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Modern Hanfu (xin hanfu or "new Hanfu") adapts traditional Hanfu elements for contemporary life. It uses simplified cuts, machine-washable fabrics, and practical silhouettes that work with modern accessories while retaining the cross-collar and layered aesthetic.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Why is Hanfu popular again?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>The Hanfu revival movement began in the early 2000s among young Chinese people seeking to reconnect with pre-Qing cultural heritage. Social media, costume dramas, and a growing global interest in traditional crafts have made Hanfu an international phenomenon.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What fabric is Hanfu made of?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Historically, Hanfu was made from silk, ramie (a type of linen), and hemp. Modern Hanfu uses silk for premium pieces, cotton and linen for everyday wear, and polyester blends for affordable, easy-care options. Brocade and gauze are popular for decorative layers.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Can kids wear Hanfu?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Yes, children's Hanfu is widely available and popular for festivals, photo shoots, and cultural events. Kids' styles are often simplified with elastic waists and softer fabrics for comfort and ease of movement. Many families enjoy matching parent-child Hanfu sets.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Can I rent Hanfu when visiting China?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Yes, Hanfu rental shops are common in major tourist cities like Xi'an, Hangzhou, Beijing, Suzhou, Chengdu, and Luoyang. Rentals cost 100-300 RMB ($14-42 USD) per day and often include basic hairstyling. Many shops near scenic areas also offer full photography packages (300-800 RMB) with edited photos delivered within 24 hours.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>Where are the best places to photograph Hanfu in China?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Top spots: Forbidden City and Summer Palace (Beijing), West Lake (Hangzhou), Ancient City Wall and Tang Paradise (Xi'an), Humble Administrator's Garden (Suzhou), Dali Ancient Town (Yunnan), and the Longmen Grottoes area (Luoyang). Visit early morning for the best lighting and minimum crowds.</p>
            </div>
          </details>

          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>What season is best for wearing Hanfu?
              <svg class="faq-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
            </summary>
            <div class="faq-answer">
              <p>Spring (March to May) and autumn (September to November) are ideal. Summer Hanfu should be lightweight cotton or linen; avoid heavy silk in hot weather. Winter Hanfu photography in northern cities requires thermal underlayers, but snowy scenes in Hangzhou or Harbin can be spectacular with the right preparation.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>

